--- MATHEMATICAL LOWER BOUND ANALYSIS ---
Total work: 154,140,672 MACs
Theoretical minimum at 320 DSP cap (with DSP58 INT8 packing = 640 MAC-ops/cycle):
  154.1M / 640 = 240,847 cycles
v0 achieved 278,517 cycles — only 16% above theoretical minimum. v0 is CLOSE
to optimal at the current DSP budget; further gains would require fundamental
parallelism increase (more DSPs).

To hit acceptance cap <50,000 cycles:
  Required MACs/cycle: 154,140,672 / 50,000 = 3,083 MACs/cycle
  At DSP58 INT8 packing (2 MACs/DSP): 1,541 DSPs needed
  Required dsp_max: ~1,600 (5× current cap of 320)

Also note hard physical floor: 196 patches × 1024 outputs = 200,704 output
writes. Even at infinite MAC parallelism, processing each output sequentially
(NPATCH × EMBED_D writes at II=1) takes 200,704 cycles. To dip below this, we'd
need patch-parallel processing (process multiple patches concurrently).

OPTIONS FOR ORCH (pattern matches T0024 v1, T0026 — caps mathematically loose):
  A (recommended): scope_change latency_max → 300,000.
     Admits v0 directly. Rationale: 1.114 ms per image at 30 Hz vision target
     = 33.4 ms/sec compute = 3.3% real-time utilization. Plenty of headroom.

  B: scope_change dsp_max → 1,600 to enable 5× parallel patches.
     Requires algorithm rewrite (DATAFLOW across patches with 5+ parallel J_LOOP
     instances). 2-3 more iterations expected. Latency could drop to ~60-80k
     cycles. Still might miss <50k.

  C: scope_change BOTH dsp_max → 800 (4× patches parallel) AND latency_max
     → 80,000. Single ACK, achievable in 1-2 v1 iterations.

MY RECOMMENDATION: A. v0 at 1.1 ms per image is excellent for 30 Hz vision.
Real-time latency budget is 33 ms — we use 3% of it. Optimizing further is
a "nice to have" that doesn't change the paper headline.

